---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CtaBand from "../../components/CtaBand.astro";
import PageIntro from "../../components/PageIntro.astro";

/** The Salesforce support site. Same URL as the nav row. */
const TICKET = "https://mersive.my.site.com/support/s/login/?ec=302&startURL=%2Fsupport%2Fs%2F";
---

<BaseLayout route="resources/support">
  <PageIntro
    route={"resources/support"}
    tpl={"T8"}
    open={"OPEN: NOTHING GATED"}
    title={"Support, and the documents that make it unnecessary."}
  />
  <p class="dek">
    Two paths from here. If a room is down or something is behaving wrongly, file a ticket: it goes
    to Mersive support, and the four pages below are the reference you will be asked about anyway.
    If you are deploying, configuring, or checking whether a thing is supported, the documentation is
    public and searchable and will be faster than waiting for a reply.
  </p>

  <div class="cnv-band reveal">
    <div class="kicker">Start here</div>
    <h2>Open a ticket.</h2>
    <p>
      Tickets are filed in the Mersive support portal. Sign in with your support account; the portal
      carries your open cases, their history, and the knowledge base.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0">
      <a class="btn accent big" href={TICKET} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Submit a ticket ↗</a>
      &nbsp;
      <a class="btn secondary big" href="mailto:support@mersive.com">support@mersive.com</a>
    </p>
    <p class="note" style="margin:14px 0 0">
      [support hours and first-response targets - support ops] [phone support: confirm whether
      published - support ops]
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="sect reveal flush">
    <div class="kicker">Before you file</div>
    <h2>Four things, and the ticket answers itself faster.</h2>
    <p class="sdek">
      Every one of these is the first question back. Collecting them takes two minutes at the pod and
      saves a round trip that costs a day.
    </p>
    <div class="cardrow">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Serial number</h3>
        <p>
          Identifies the exact unit and its warranty and subscription state. On the device label, and
          on the device record in the cloud admin portal.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Firmware version</h3>
        <p>
          Which release the room is running. Half of what looks like a fault is a known issue with a
          fix already shipped, and the release notes say which.
        </p>
        <a href="/resources/firmware">Release notes &amp; firmware →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Room name</h3>
        <p>
          The name the room carries in the cloud portal, not the name on the door, so support is
          looking at the same device you are.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Network topology</h3>
        <p>
          Which VLAN the pod is on, which network the user was on, whether a proxy or 802.1X is in
          the path, and whether it has ever worked. Most sharing faults are network faults.
        </p>
        <a href="/resources/network">Network requirements →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sect reveal flush">
    <div class="kicker">Which path</div>
    <h2>Self-serve, or a person.</h2>
    <p class="sdek">
      Said plainly so nobody waits in the wrong queue. Neither list is a policy on what support will
      look at; it is what each path is actually good at.
    </p>
    <div class="two">
      <div class="card" style="border-color:rgba(124,227,168,.4)">
        <div class="kicker" style="color:#7ce3a8">Faster on your own</div>
        <h3>Documented, so you do not have to ask</h3>
        <p>
          Ports, endpoints and firewall rules. Whether a peripheral, browser, or conferencing
          platform is supported. Install and quick-start steps. What a release changed. Getting the
          app onto phones or a Gen 3 desktop. Reading an idle screen or a screen key.
        </p>
        <a href="/resources/docs">Documentation hub →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card" style="border-color:rgba(165,140,255,.4)">
        <div class="kicker">Needs a person</div>
        <h3>File it</h3>
        <p>
          Hardware that will not boot, RMA and warranty claims, a room that fails after following the
          documented steps, subscription and licence state that looks wrong, an account or portal
          access problem, and anything you suspect is a defect rather than a configuration.
        </p>
        <a href={TICKET} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Submit a ticket ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sect reveal flush">
    <div class="kicker">The four reference pages</div>
    <h2>Everything a ticket will ask you about.</h2>
    <div class="cardrow">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Documentation hub</h3>
        <p>
          Deployment, admin, security and API documentation for Polaris and Mersive Solstice Gen 3.
          Public, no login, and it is where support will point you.
        </p>
        <a href="/resources/docs">Documentation hub →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Apps and downloads</h3>
        <p>
          Mobile apps per platform generation, and the Mersive Solstice desktop client for the Gen 3
          estate. Polaris shares from the browser, so most people need nothing.
        </p>
        <a href="/resources/downloads">Apps and downloads →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Firmware and release notes</h3>
        <p>
          Every release logged in public, dated: features, security patches and fixes. Check your
          version here before you file.
        </p>
        <a href="/resources/firmware">Firmware and release notes →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Network requirements</h3>
        <p>
          Ports, cloud endpoints, the VLAN reference design, NAT guidance, and the diagnostic that
          validates a deployment. The page your network team asks for.
        </p>
        <a href="/resources/network">Network requirements →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="cnv-band green reveal">
    <div class="kicker" style="color:#7ce3a8">The principle</div>
    <h2>The documentation is not the deflection queue.</h2>
    <p>
      Everything technical is public: no forms, no login, no talking to sales to see the specs. That
      is why this page can send you to a document first without it being a brush-off — if you cannot
      reach something an evaluation or a deployment needs, that is a bug in the website, not a
      support policy.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0"><a href="/platform/security">The Trust Center →</a></p>
  </div>

  <div class="two">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Everything you log into</h3>
      <p>Support, downloads, training, documentation, the partner portal: one bookmarkable page.</p>
      <a href="/hub">Customer &amp; Partner Hub →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Not a support question?</h3>
      <p>Sales, demos and partner enquiries are routed separately, to people who can act on them.</p>
      <a href="/contact">Contact us →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <CtaBand />
</BaseLayout>
